<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moravian Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Unitas Fratrum, Moravian Brethren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moravian Church, or Unitas Fratrum, is a Protestant church descended from the Bohemian Brethren of the 15th century. Refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> renewed it in 1722 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, on the estate of Count Nicolaus von Zinzendorf. Known for communal settlements, hymn singing and overseas missions, the Brethren were welcomed by tolerant German rulers such as the counts of Wied-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> and Isenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moravian-church.jpg" alt="Moravian Church" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JJackman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AgnusDeiWindow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion shaped society deeply: the Hussite revolution transferred much church land to nobles and towns, and for two centuries Utraquists, who gave communion in both kinds, coexisted with Catholics, later joined by the <b>Unity of the Brethren</b> and Lutherans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The worldwide <b>Moravian Church</b> takes its name from Brethren refugees who left Moravia for Herrnhut in Saxony in 1722; its missionaries reached the Caribbean by 1732.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1712 Count Ernst Casimir issued an edict of tolerance that drew Inspirationists, <b>Moravian Brethren</b> and other dissenters to the Ronneburg and Marienborn.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-isenburg-budingen">County of Isenburg-Büdingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Neuwied welcomed Mennonites, <b>Moravian Brethren</b>, Catholics, Jews and Reformed Christians, each with its own congregation.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wied-neuwied">County of Wied-Neuwied</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf, founder of the renewed <b>Moravian Church</b> at Herrnhut, married Erdmuthe Dorothea of Reuss-Ebersdorf in 1722, and a Moravian congregation settled in Ebersdorf.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-ebersdorf">Principality of Reuss-Ebersdorf</a></li></ul></section>
